    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d612">

                <pb facs="0557" n="550" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg550"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/10244: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-09-25T16:00:00+08:00">September 25, 1937—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 25—7 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>712. Under instructions from the Military Attaché, Captain Roberts<anchor n="53"
                        xml:id="d612-f1"/> asked the General Staff to obtain permission from the
                    Field Commander at the Shanghai Front for Captain Sutherland<anchor n="54"
                        xml:id="d612-f2"/> to visit Shanghai hostilities as a military observer.
                    Field Commander refused. On September 13 I wrote informally to General Chiang
                    strongly urging reconsideration. I received no reply even though I indirectly
                    brought influence to bear. On September 23 I brought the matter again to the
                    attention of General Chiang through Donald<anchor n="55" xml:id="d612-f3"/> but
                    latter informed me by letter that Chiang replied Chinese officers at the front
                    could not assume risk of possible injuries to a foreign officer and refusal was
                    definite. Unless Department instructs otherwise I shall not renew attempt.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d612-f1" n="53" xml:id="d612-f1-content">
                <p>Capt. Frank N. Roberts, U. S. A., language officer in China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d612-f2" n="54" xml:id="d612-f2-content">
                <p>Capt. Edwin M. Sutherland, U. S. A., language officer in China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d612-f3" n="55" xml:id="d612-f3-content">
                <p>W. H. Donald, Australian adviser to Generalissimo Chiang Kai-shek.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
